WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.100
SIERRA: So, like many of you, we at Why It
Matters have been watching the news coming

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:13.570
out of Afghanistan. The United States 20-year
war has come to an end, the government we

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:19.369
attempted to build there has failed, and the
Taliban has retaken the country with breathtaking

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:27.640
speed. The footage is haunting - images of
people desperately clinging to an airplane

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:37.649
as it takes off from Kabul, women expressing
fear of the new regime. For many of us it

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:47.000
s a time of reckoning, and of soul-searching
about what went wrong. The debate is going

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.270
to continue for years to come, but one thing
is certain, we have to try to learn the right

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:58.690
lessons from Afghanistan. So, in an effort
to start learning those lessons, we thought

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:03.240
we would do something different this week.
Usually on the show we feature several guests

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.870
with differing opinions. We think that s important.
But today we are just going to have one guest,

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:15.720
and he s my boss, Richard Haass, the President
of the Council on Foreign Relations. We spoke

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.770
on zoom, and the team decided to release the
conversation with minimal editing. The views

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:25.770
you will hear are his own. We spoke to him
because he has spent four decades studying

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:31.210
and advising on Afghanistan, serving under
four presidents. He was in the room when many

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:38.390
of the most important early decisions about
the Afghanistan War were debated and shaped.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:47.979
There s a lot on his resume, but here are
a few things you should know. He was in the

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:52.810
State Department in the 1980s when the Reagan
Administration attempted to counter the Soviet

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.920
invasion of Afghanistan. He later worked at
the National Security Council under the first

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.080
President Bush, where he attempted to implement
a post-Soviet policy for the country. Most

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.329
importantly, he was the head of policy planning
for the State Department under the second

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:13.480
President Bush when 9/11 occurred, and became
the U.S. Coordinator for the future of Afghanistan

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.849
in the period that followed. He left the Bush
administration in 2003 to become the President

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:26.840
of CFR. I m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is
Why It Matters. Today, Richard Haass on the

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.760
Afghanistan War.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.202
SIERRA: Welcome to the show, Richard Haass.
So, it's quite a timeline you've had. An incredible

00:02:34.202 --> 00:02:36.080
amount of experience this whole way. So to
dive into some of these pieces, you know let's

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:42.000
go back a bit. Based on the reality at the
time, do you think we were right to go into

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Afghanistan?

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.129
HAASS: We were 100% right to go into it, and,
we didn't want to do it. We gave the Taliban

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:51.051
a choice. The Taliban made the wrong choice,
a fateful choice for them. 3,000 people have

00:02:51.051 --> 00:02:55.379
died in our country in a day. But also the
precedent. We thought it was critical to set

00:02:55.379 --> 00:03:00.159
the international norm that we wouldn't distinguish
between terrorists and those who supported

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:05.060
them. So I think that was 100% right. We hadn't
thought through, a whole lot, what we would

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.019
do afterwards. We didn't know how long this
would take. But then again I think we made

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:13.239
the right decision to help the Afghans forge
their own government. The feeling was if we

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:17.470
did too much, it would lack legitimacy. We
thought it was important to come up with an

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:22.410
Afghan leader, also who was Pashtun, not from
one of the northern tribes. And Hamid Karzai

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:28.221
emerged as that person. That was the only
thing that was clear. After that, and I expect

00:03:28.221 --> 00:03:31.680
we'll talk about it, there wasn't a clear
plan and there wasn't consensus within the

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:38.180
administration over what ought to be the trajectory
of US policy. How ambitious should we be?

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:42.660
What was our definition of success at that
point? And that's where things began to break

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:43.660
down.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.549
SIERRA: I mean, I remember this early phase
a bit. You know, how effective were we at

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:54.069
diminishing the terrorist threat to the United
States? Because I remember, at the time, everyone

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:55.900
was talking about the War on Terror.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.599
HAASS: Well, the War on Terror became an important
part of American foreign policy. But the Afghan

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:06.560
dimension of it had been dramatically reduced.
And the War on Terror became a global preoccupation,

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.480
particularly in parts of the Middle East and
Africa, because that's where various cells

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:16.930
of terrorists set up shop. And what we discovered,
groups like Al-Qaeda and others, they were

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:22.280
global. They had access to money. They had
access to guns. They had access to people.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:28.400
And that phase of American foreign policy
began 20-odd years ago, and it's still happening.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.639
It's still taking place, largely but not exclusively,
in the Middle East and Africa. It's also taking

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:37.270
place in parts of Asia. And, to me, it's open-ended.
I mean, here we are, we're meeting at a time

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:42.230
we're battling a disease. I think there's
some parallels. There's an open-ended quality.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:47.229
You don't eliminate terrorism anymore than
you eliminate a virus. These are now baked

00:04:47.229 --> 00:04:52.780
into the world of the 21st century. So I think
it's an open-ended challenge, though for a

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:58.990
moment, where it had come to us most painfully
and vividly, was out of Afghanistan. I should

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:03.600
be clear for those who don't know, the history
of that is where the terrorists involved in

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.090
9/11 had been trained, where they had gotten
support. They weren't Afghans, most of them

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:11.400
were from Saudi Arabia and elsewhere in the
Middle East. But this is where they had been

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.620
supported. This is where things were organized.
This is where Osama Bin Laden was hanging

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:22.911
out. But with the removal of the Taliban pushing
the Al-Qaeda out and the Taliban out, most

00:05:22.911 --> 00:05:28.360
of whom went to Pakistan, Afghanistan was
no longer the epicenter of world terrorism.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.370
It essentially had dispersed.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:36.950
SIERRA: So, how did our reason for being in
Afghanistan change through the years? You

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:40.960
know, what were the competing views on what
should be done after the initial phase?

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.100
HAASS: It's the most important question and
I'm glad you asked it. Because it's something

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:50.729
that comes back to haunt us in many ways.
So we had successfully worked with our Afghan

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:55.350
partners, removed the old authority, we had
helped bring about a new authority, led by

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.800
Mr. Karzai. And then the question was, well
what do you do at that point? They weren't

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:04.669
strong. The feeling was we couldn't just leave
them to be, because we knew that the Taliban

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:09.360
had moved to Pakistan. But the question was,
how do we stand them up? How do we build them

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.960
up so they could be something approximating
a normal country? We weren't talking about

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:18.569
democracy at this point, we were talking about
building up the capacity of this post-Taliban

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:23.160
government so they could police their borders.
And they could police their country so that

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:29.319
terrorists would not once again use Afghanistan
as a piece of real estate. And the debates

00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:34.930
we had within the Bush Administration, again
this is Bush 43, and I remember them painfully

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:39.400
and vividly was how, to put it bluntly how
ambitious should we be? And one of the phrases

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:44.310
that's commonly now booted about is nation
building. And what that really is, is capacity

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:49.720
building. What kind of capacities or sometimes
called state building ought we to try to bring

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:55.730
about? And what I was proposing was that the
United States and other international forces

00:06:55.730 --> 00:07:01.599
would come to Afghanistan temporarily. We
already had some. We would stay there temporarily

00:07:01.599 --> 00:07:06.190
and perform two functions. One was we would
help the new government consolidate authority

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:13.160
over Afghan real estate, it's a large country.
And secondly, we would help develop, train,

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:16.940
a force. Wasn't clear how national it would
be as opposed to regional, but we would help

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:23.260
stand up an Afghan army. All I can say is
there was remarkably little enthusiasm within

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:28.330
the US government for doing it, remarkably
little. I'm used to being unsuccessful in

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:34.789
my policy proposals, but even in my career
of unsuccessful attempts to influence policy

00:07:34.789 --> 00:07:39.560
this stood out. It was one of the most painful
national security meetings I had ever attended.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:44.349
There just wasn't enthusiasm. If I had to
boil it down Gabrielle, I would say it reflected

00:07:44.349 --> 00:07:50.629
two things. One was pretty legitimate, which
was why do we wanna get ambitious in Afghanistan?

00:07:50.629 --> 00:07:56.030
This is a country with little tradition of
a strong central government. It's very tribal,

00:07:56.030 --> 00:08:01.360
it's regional. This is just the wrong place
for us to get ambitious. And I think that

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.170
was a legitimate concern. The other concern,
less legitimate, is there was much more excitement

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:10.909
about getting involved more in other parts
of the world i.e. Iraq. And the feeling was

00:08:10.909 --> 00:08:14.991
that that was a place where if we did invest
we would have more to show for it. Iraq was

00:08:14.991 --> 00:08:21.129
a potential democracy. Iraq was a model that
other Arab countries might emulate. Afghanistan

00:08:21.129 --> 00:08:26.361
was seen as an isolated one-off. So it was
not only seen as a poor prospect, which again

00:08:26.361 --> 00:08:31.780
I think was a legitimate concern, it was seen
as a poor investment. What I said was we had

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:37.399
a window, the Taliban had been routed, our
authority was really high. We needed to build

00:08:37.399 --> 00:08:41.830
up authority. You can't remove an authority
and then not try to put something in its place.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:47.080
And for those who question me on that I have
one word for you, Libya. Under the Obama presidency,

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:52.260
the United States went in and removed Gaddafi
and never put anything in his place and Libya

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:57.460
became a failed state. And what we learned
in some cases is that bad situations can get

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:01.610
worse. So my view on Afghanistan, I didn't
have that example at the time, was to say

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:05.990
we've gotta try to do something. Let's not
get overly ambitious, but let's also not get

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:09.950
under ambitious. We have a moment here. And
I thought we would have significant international

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:13.589
help, and I said for a short time we could
do this, but there was just simply no enthusiasm

00:09:13.589 --> 00:09:17.920
for it. And if you look at the history, what
happened subsequently is the rate of if you

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:24.250
will nation building or capacity building
or army building in Afghanistan, it was incredibly

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:31.079
slow, glacial may be an exaggeration but not
too much of one. Meanwhile, this was not happening

00:09:31.079 --> 00:09:34.820
in isolation. What else was happening? So
we were building up the Afghans at I think

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:40.850
a terribly slow rate, but the Taliban was
rebuilding and reconstituting at a pretty

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:41.850
good clip.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:42.850
SIERRA: Right.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:46.110
HAASS: Why? Because they had set up shop in
Pakistan. And whereas initially we had gone

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:49.030
to the Pakistanis, it turned out one of the
senior members of the Pakistani government

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:53.240
had been in Washington on 9/11. And we read
them the Riot Act that the State Department,

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:58.110
Rich Armitage, the deputy secretary. And initially
the Pakistanis cleaned up their act, but over

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:04.660
time things kind of went back to business
as usual. And you had the Taliban openly operating

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:10.820
out of Pakistani cities. And the North-West
Frontier Province of Pakistan became a giant

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:15.970
sanctuary for the Taliban. And one of the
things history says, it's very hard to prevail

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:21.760
in a civil war if one of the parties has access
to a cross-border sanctuary. Which is exactly

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:28.580
what happened. So we had two dynamics, a very
slow dynamic of building up government capacity,

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:35.300
and a rather healthy dynamic or fast dynamic,
which was unhealthy, of the Taliban reconstituting

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:40.050
itself. In part because the US military had
failed to stop them from escaping, and then

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:46.900
we kind of took our eye off the ball and Pakistan
went ahead and allowed the Taliban to regroup

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:53.430
and rebuild. So that's where we ended up.
By then, I had left government years before

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:58.060
in the summer of 2003 to take my current job.
But this is what happened, over the course

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:02.540
of the Bush years. And then by the time the
Obama administration got in then the Taliban

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:08.019
had resumed all sorts of efforts within Afghanistan.
The situation was beginning to deteriorate,

00:11:08.019 --> 00:11:12.460
and by then we made the fateful decision to
dramatically increase American forces. And

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:17.420
the problem with surging US forces is one,
by then we clearly had overstayed our welcome

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:22.870
in Afghanistan. Two, as I said we had allowed
the Taliban to rebuild. And three, the fact

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:29.750
that we were surging forces increasingly involved
in combat against a reconstituted Taliban

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:35.029
meant that US casualties went way up and the
cost of the war in every definition of the

00:11:35.029 --> 00:11:41.200
word cost went way up. So Afghanistan went
from being the good war to simply the second

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:45.980
bad war. And that happened during the time
of the Obama administration.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:51.550
SIERRA: So, you testified before The Senate
Foreign Relations Committee and expressed

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:56.360
your skepticism that the surge in Afghanistan
was a good choice. I think at the time, you

00:11:56.360 --> 00:12:01.870
said, that we may have to accept a situation
that is just good enough in Afghanistan.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:08.000
HAASS: The surge was a kind of an attempt
to be decisive, in a situation I didn't think

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:13.050
we could be decisive. That it wasn't gonna
end in military victory, it wasn't gonna bring

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:18.070
about the Taliban yelling uncle for peace.
So, I favored over the years, was something

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:23.269
more modest. The idea of Afghanistan good
enough? or Iraq, good enough? I actually think

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:29.510
is an important one. Americans tend to see
situations as problems, and anytime one hears

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:35.639
the word "problem," one immediately expects
to see the word "solution." And the problem

00:12:35.639 --> 00:12:40.800
with thinking of things as problems, now that
I've totally muddled it, is that lots of things

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:47.550
are really situations, and by definition,
situations cannot be solved with military

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:53.600
force or any other policies. At best, they
can be managed. Which often means not what

00:12:53.600 --> 00:13:00.639
you can bring about, but what it is you can
avoid. I wanted to dial down our ambitions

00:13:00.639 --> 00:13:07.610
and simply say, here's what we want, we want
to avoid a Taliban takeover of the major cities.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:12.660
Can't stop the Taliban from making some inroads.
So, what we wanna focus on is a situation

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:18.769
we can help sustain at an affordable cost.
This argues against the surge. A surge was

00:13:18.769 --> 00:13:25.020
looking for a decisive blow in a situation
where there simply was not a decisive blow.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:31.959
I think we've gotta be modest about what we
ask military force to do. I have Colin Powell,

00:13:31.959 --> 00:13:34.930
before he was Secretary of State, as you know,
he was a General, and he said, you know, military

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:40.140
force is good at destroying things. What military
force can do is create a context in which

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:45.050
other things can happen, but my own view is,
we often turn to it too often. But, look,

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:46.050
going back to your immediate question, yeah,
I traffic in ideas, like anybody else, I look

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.171
at history. I try to make sense of what's
going on. I offer recommendations, and whether

00:13:47.171 --> 00:13:48.171
I'm in government or out of government. And,
you know, I've spent decades in government

00:13:48.171 --> 00:13:49.171
and decades out of government. It's not only
that I'm wrong sometimes and perhaps right

00:13:49.171 --> 00:13:50.171
sometimes, but also, you know, there's times
you get listened to and times you don't. And

00:13:50.171 --> 00:13:51.171
it's just frustrating when the times, it's
not that you don't get listened to. It's on

00:13:51.171 --> 00:13:52.171
those occasions where you might have been
more right than wrong, and the consequences

00:13:52.171 --> 00:13:53.171
of what we did are really costly. Those are
the things that keep me up at night, and Afghanistan

00:13:53.171 --> 00:13:54.171
falls into that bucket. There's things about
Afghanistan and we're seeing them play out

00:13:54.171 --> 00:13:55.171
before us now, that if you're not frustrated
by them, if you're not made a little bit sick

00:13:55.171 --> 00:13:56.171
by them, by what we're seeing, then you're
not paying attention.

00:13:56.171 --> 00:13:57.220
SIERRA: I guess that brings me to a question
of does nation building ever work?

00:13:57.220 --> 00:14:02.339
HAASS: Sure. I mean the most famous successes
are places like Germany and Japan after World

00:14:02.339 --> 00:14:03.339
War II.

00:14:03.339 --> 00:14:08.079
HAASS: These were defeated, then occupied
places. So the question you have to ask is

00:14:08.079 --> 00:14:13.490
why did it work there? And you look at the
characteristics of these societies, highly

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:20.040
educated, highly homogenous, in many cases,
societies with strong national traditions

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:25.509
and so forth. None of these things were present
in Afghanistan. So again it was not a good

00:14:25.509 --> 00:14:31.339
candidate, I get it. The question was, I didn't
see clear alternatives at that point. Now

00:14:31.339 --> 00:14:36.199
I couldn't guarantee it would work. But I
thought it was worth a limited investment.

00:14:36.199 --> 00:14:40.380
I didn't think it was high risk at that moment
because we had tremendous authority, we had

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:46.060
tremendous momentum, the Taliban were gone,
Al-Qaeda was gone. So I thought there was

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:51.070
a window. And my view was let's take advantage
of this window. Worse comes to worst it won't

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:56.449
work. Okay, then we can deal with the consequences
of that. But we were not willing to give it

00:14:56.449 --> 00:15:03.130
I thought a serious try, and that set in motion
a situation where the government of Afghanistan

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:09.589
was never able to do what it needed to do.
We misguidedly took on an ever larger role.

00:15:09.589 --> 00:15:13.560
So we became not just the nation builders
behind the scenes, we essentially became a

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:19.820
protagonist in their civil war. And that seemed
to me an escalation that was unwise. But I'm

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:24.620
worried here, that wars are always fought
three times. There's the question of whether

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:29.509
you fight a war, there's the fighting of the
war and then it's the lessons. And what worries

00:15:29.509 --> 00:15:33.139
me a little bit about this is that we could
learn the wrong lessons. I don't think the

00:15:33.139 --> 00:15:39.450
lesson ought to be that nation or state building
is always wrong, it's always destined to fail.

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:43.670
I think what we really need to think hard
about is what are the conditions which we

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:49.050
think are positive, and what are the techniques?
What have we learned about sequencing? What

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:55.600
have we learned about pacing? What have we
learned about how do we adjust for local culture

00:15:55.600 --> 00:16:00.199
and history? And the reason this is so important,
we don't want to be doing everything ourselves

00:16:00.199 --> 00:16:04.920
around the world. But there's dozens of governments
around the world we need to help, and particularly

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:11.650
in the Middle East, particularly in Central
America, particularly in Africa. And so we

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:17.010
had better learn some of the correct lessons
of nation building rather than to say well

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:21.460
it's never worth it, or it's never a good
idea. That seems to me we either live in a

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:26.380
world which is much more dangerous, or we
confront ourselves with having the United

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:31.980
States get involved directly in combat operations
in more places, and I sure don't want that.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:37.449
SIERRA: I mean look, one of the things that
I can't help but think about is, you know,

00:16:37.449 --> 00:16:41.529
the women, right. I mean, this is something
that has been told to us in the messaging

00:16:41.529 --> 00:16:47.120
of why we were even still in Afghanistan,
the repression of women. And these women saw

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:51.790
a level of freedom, and now they saw this
chance disappearing. You know, you mention

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:56.130
culture, what responsibilities do we have
to a society and to a culture when we come

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.130
into a country and leave?

00:16:58.130 --> 00:17:01.690
HAASS: You're asking big questions today,
and I'm not going to avoid your question,

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:06.199
but it's not an easy one. Look, this is one
of the fundamental debates in American foreign

00:17:06.199 --> 00:17:12.000
policy. To what extent should American foreign
policy be about shaping and influencing the

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:17.110
external behavior of other countries? Essentially,
to what extent should American foreign policy

00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:21.640
be about influencing the foreign policy of
others? And to what extent should what we

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:27.791
do in the world be about influencing the domestic
behavior, the nature of others? The problem

00:17:27.791 --> 00:17:33.660
is with that, often our influence is limited.
Often we have other priorities. Maybe the

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:40.390
single most ambitious task you can imagine
you can set for yourself, is to try to change

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:45.100
the internal workings of another society,
particularly one with a long and deep culture.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:50.380
So I take from this, yeah, we have values
here, but we've got to understand in some

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:54.610
cases the limits to our influence, that we
can't always translate our preferences. I

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:58.870
don't like this. I still remember a dinner
party where I made this argument about the

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:05.480
Middle East, and I was excoriated, I think
is not too strong of a word. Hammered, might

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:12.520
be another word by a prominent former policy
maker on the basis of, I was selling people

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:19.870
short in the Middle East. And I was actually
accused of a kind of racism, that not everybody

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:23.531
was ready for democracy. And I said I wasn't
making a statement about individuals. I was

00:18:23.531 --> 00:18:28.289
making a statement about cultures and societies.
And you know, I'd studied enough anthropology

00:18:28.289 --> 00:18:32.870
and sociology and enough about the Middle
East in particular where one had to be aware

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:40.000
of pre-conditions and resistance to certain
types of traditions. And I take that as simply,

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:44.750
I don't like it. I like the idea that everybody
in the Middle East would read the Federalist

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:49.790
Papers, an Arabic translation, and that people
in Afghanistan would be reading it in Dari

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:55.179
or whatever, but it's not necessarily going
to happen. So, I think we have to decide what

00:18:55.179 --> 00:18:58.820
are the limits to our influence, what are
our priorities. What are those things that

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:03.283
American foreign policy is well suited to
do? What are those things that are, that are

00:19:03.283 --> 00:19:11.169
a reach? We're about to have a clinic in that
in Afghanistan because look, I worry profoundly

00:19:11.169 --> 00:19:16.310
about what's gonna happen, yes, to girls and
women, first and foremost, but really to Afghans

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:20.350
of any gender, and not just those who are
working with the government or with us, about

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:26.679
people who want to have a 21st century life.
I worry and wonder. And, I worry about what's

00:19:26.679 --> 00:19:31.150
going on in other parts of the world. Afghanistan
is not unique in the sense of those kinds

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:36.560
of concerns, but we can't impose our preferences
on others. I think the best thing we can often

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:41.130
do is our example. And our example, by the
way, is not what it could and should be right

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:46.400
now. So, whether it's to take an example of
our democracy, or the example of our economy,

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:50.550
the example of, our physical health, our social
health. I mean, there's so many ways we can

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:57.370
make this a society worth admiring and emulating.
That might be the best way in many cases we

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:02.820
can spread our values, but I don't think we're
in a position. Look, we couldn't convince

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:07.140
Myanmar. Think about the discrepancy between
the power of the United States and the power

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:13.400
of Myanmar. We can't oust the military junta
that took over there. Doesn't that tell people

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:19.440
something? There's a limit to our influence
in the world. And you know, we may be on the

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:27.220
side of right and morals and virtue, but that's
not necessarily the way history plays out.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:34.039
SIERRA: But it's such a fine line, isn't it,
between spreading and setting a good example,

00:20:34.039 --> 00:20:35.039
and imposing?

00:20:35.039 --> 00:20:39.740
HAASS: So, you're right. And I don't mean
to caricature. So there are things we can

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:44.320
do. And we can, through foreign aid, do certain
things, through sanctions, discourage certain

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:50.390
behaviors. Through educational opportunities
here, we can do things. But there's still

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:55.010
limits. If the Taliban wants to impose Sharia
Law, wants to make, wants to force women to

00:20:55.010 --> 00:21:00.400
wear a niqab, full covering. I don't think
we're gonna be in a position to stop it. Now

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:03.860
we might say, "Well because of these things,
we're not gonna provide you with this or that

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:08.690
kind of aid." Or, okay, and they may simply
shrug their shoulders and say, "So be it,"

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.730
and they may be able to find the support they
need externally from other places, be it Pakistan

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:18.330
or China or Russia, who tend not to care about
such things and have other priorities. So

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:23.540
again, we can have a foreign policy in part
which tries to more directly promote certain

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:29.039
behaviors, certain norms, certain standards,
but let me ask you a question. Just say what

00:21:29.039 --> 00:21:33.890
should we care most about in Afghanistan?
And the priority has been that it not become

00:21:33.890 --> 00:21:37.970
a place where terrorists again operate out
of. That's got to be the single most important

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:43.350
thing. That, if you will, is a more classic
foreign policy interest. And as we showed

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:49.130
on 9/11, after 9/11, we have mechanisms to
act with if that standard is not met by the

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:55.440
Taliban. They may have internalized that lesson.
We'll see whether they have internalized that.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:01.799
But no one is gonna launch an intervention
in Afghanistan over Sharia Law or the fact

00:22:01.799 --> 00:22:07.420
that women are treated abominably, as bad
as that is. So, again, it's one of the reasons

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:11.539
I didn't want us to get to this point. It's
one of the reasons, that I favored the United

00:22:11.539 --> 00:22:17.470
States maintaining a small presence in Afghanistan
not because it would mean peace, not because

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:21.160
it would lead to a military victory, but I
thought it would avoid some of the scenes

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:26.580
we're either seeing or are likely to see in
coming days, weeks, months, and years. And

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:33.320
that, to me, was a consideration, the fate
that would likely befall girls and women,

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:35.029
as well as men in Afghanistan.

00:22:35.029 --> 00:22:39.850
SIERRA: Yeah. It just feels sometimes like
a very fair weather argument where, you know,

00:22:39.850 --> 00:22:44.590
people have used that defense to stay in Afghanistan
in the past, and now we can get up and leave

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:46.130
and abandon everybody.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:49.870
HAASS: Well, it will have consequences. You
know, I've disagreed with this president's

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:54.790
policy on Afghanistan, as you know, from what
I've said and what I've written. His argument

00:22:54.790 --> 00:23:01.010
was that he was not prepared, as gut wrenching
as it is, to have American forces stay in

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:06.830
Afghanistan longer, potentially put themselves
in harm's way, in order to deal with the issues

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:11.240
you're raising. That it was one thing to deal
with Afghanistan as a terrorist haven. It's

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:14.640
something else to deal with it as a human
rights nightmare. And he was basically saying,

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:20.080
we will turn to diplomacy. Well quite honestly
diplomacy ain't gonna do a lot. Mr. Biden

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:24.570
had inherited a situation that was a kind
of cake and eat it situation. We had a small

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:29.169
military presence. Americans hadn't been doing
combat operations, I don't know what, for

00:23:29.169 --> 00:23:33.880
six, seven, eight years now. I've lost count.
Hadn't had a combat fatality for a year and

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:39.789
a half. That we had managed to get to a point
where the benefits and the costs were not

00:23:39.789 --> 00:23:44.340
out of line, and one of the benefits are the
issues you've raised, the quality of life

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:50.779
in particular for Afghan girls and women.
The president wasn't willing to take the risk

00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:55.669
that the costs of presence would go up. He
obviously didn't want to face the decision

00:23:55.669 --> 00:24:00.870
of having to increase US forces if the security
situation deteriorated, so he's essentially

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:05.960
initiated a policy that's brought about a
truly, I think a set of terrible outcomes.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:09.870
And my guess is he would say, "I don't like
these outcomes anymore than you did. I was

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:14.380
just not willing to take the risk of what
the price would be of our staying." And look,

00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:18.059
I'm not gonna sit here and say that's not
a legitimate set of concerns. I actually think

00:24:18.059 --> 00:24:22.400
there's legitimate debate, not over the way
the withdrawal was designed and implemented.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:26.370
That was terrible. But I think there is a
legitimate debate, about whether the agreement

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:32.240
signed by the Trump administration in February
of 2020.I think that was a terrible agreement,

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:36.590
to get us out and undercut the government,
it virtually asked nothing of the Taliban.

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:42.250
I was the US Envoy to the Northern Ireland
peace talks. We asked much more of the provisional

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:46.890
IRA in Northern Ireland than we ever asked
of the Taliban. Just saying. We demanded a

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:51.080
cease fire. We demanded they give up their
arms. We didn't either with the Taliban. The

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:56.350
Taliban got off so, so, so easily. This was
not a peace agreement. This was an American

00:24:56.350 --> 00:25:00.110
withdrawal agreement. I thought the Trump
administration was dead wrong to do it. I

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:04.110
thought Mr. Biden, who's had no trouble distancing
himself from other things he inherited from

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:09.190
Mr. Trump on Iran, on climate change, on the
World Health Organization, should have distanced

00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:12.929
himself from this, but he didn't. Essentially,
followed it.

00:25:12.929 --> 00:25:18.070
SIERRA: But public opinion, everyone wanted,
you know, mostly did want to get out, right?

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:22.289
HAASS: Well I didn't see a lot of intensity.
Sure, if you ask people, do you want to get

00:25:22.289 --> 00:25:25.720
out, they'll probably say they want to get
out of most places. Unless I missed it, most

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:29.610
people, when they ask people, why did you
vote the way you voted in November '20 I didn't

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:30.840
notice people saying Afghanistan.

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:31.840
SIERRA: Right, right.

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:37.169
HAASS: The protests we had in, '20 and '21
were about race issues, policing issues. We're

00:25:37.169 --> 00:25:39.470
not about Afghanistan. This was not the Vietnam
War.

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:40.470
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:44.860
HAASS: So, you know, again, policy is not
necessarily made on the idea of popularity.

00:25:44.860 --> 00:25:49.179
That's why we don't have referendums every
day in this country. Well, we have a representative

00:25:49.179 --> 00:25:51.990
government, where leaders make tough decisions.
The fact that Americans may today say they

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:55.720
like the fact we're out, well how much would
they like it if in a couple years we face

00:25:55.720 --> 00:26:00.920
problems of terrorism? How much will they
like it if human rights atrocities happen?

00:26:00.920 --> 00:26:04.750
I don't think you can conduct foreign policy
on the basis of short-term popularity.

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:10.700
SIERRA: I'm sure you don't love that people
neglect foreign policy a bit in their day-to-day

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:14.910
decisions and their voting, and then focus
on this type of thing.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:22.940
HAASS: Look, it is, what it is, what it is.
And I wish there were more consistent interest

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:28.000
in the world, particularly since we're living
in a world. Coming back to the theme of this

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:34.429
podcast you host. You don't have to look very
far to see how the world matters. We're up

00:26:34.429 --> 00:26:40.190
against the 20th anniversary of 9/11. Where
3,000 people died here in a day. We've been

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:46.510
talking about on your show, COVID. More than
600,000 Americans already have lost their

00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:51.460
lives from a disease that broke out in Wuhan,
China, however it broke out. We are dealing

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:57.279
with the growing terrible effects of climate
change, which is, if you will, the cumulative

00:26:57.279 --> 00:27:02.520
effect of activity, everywhere. What we put
in cyberspace now is no longer safe. We can't

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:06.700
even protect our own cyberspace from other
people putting things in there we don't want.

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:11.649
HAASS: So, people may not be that interested
in the world, but the world's interested in

00:27:11.649 --> 00:27:17.270
us. That seems to me simply a fact of life.
And the reason foreign policy's so important

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:21.820
is what we do and don't do have, in turn an
impact on the world. There's a loop here.

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:25.960
The world influences us. We can influence
the world. And I think what's interesting

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:31.231
about Afghanistan, if you look at the last
20 years, I think there's been, moments, some

00:27:31.231 --> 00:27:36.570
moments we got it right, I think after, again,
9/11, I think we got it initially right. I

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:40.600
think along the ways, we've done too little
and we've done too much. You almost, I almost,

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:43.360
wish you'd invited Goldilocks on your show
instead of me.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:44.710
SIERRA: Ah, you know, she was so busy today.
I am sorry about that.

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:45.710
HAASS: Yeah, she tends to be overbooked.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:46.710
SIERRA: Yeah, you know. Yeah.

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:47.710
HAASS: That Goldie, she just doesn't say no.
It's too bad.

00:27:47.710 --> 00:27:49.269
SIERRA: A real people pleaser.

00:27:49.269 --> 00:27:54.610
HAASS: But, if you look at the history of
the last 20 years, you could almost form two

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:59.419
columns, the times we have overreached in
Afghanistan, and the times we have under-reached.

00:27:59.419 --> 00:28:04.510
And the last couple of, I'd say the last 18
months, there's been a lot of under-reaching.

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:10.899
What I was talking about, I think, after,
right after 9/11, early on in 2001, 2, 3,

00:28:10.899 --> 00:28:16.019
I think we under-reached in some of the nation-building.
Controversial, I get it. But clearly with

00:28:16.019 --> 00:28:20.970
the surge and other things, we overreached.
We put too many forces, but that's the kind

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:26.250
of conversation we need. I think it's important
to come away with the right lessons. The only

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:29.019
thing worse than making mistakes is not learning
from them.

00:28:29.019 --> 00:28:34.299
SIERRA: Well, on that note, Richard Haass,
thank you for joining us on Why It Matters

00:28:34.299 --> 00:28:35.299
today.

00:28:35.299 --> 00:28:37.520
HAASS: Always a pleasure. Good to see you.

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:42.309
SIERRA: Good to see you as well.

00:28:42.309 --> 00:28:50.419
